{
  "title": "The shape of a useful control",
  "duration": 50.729,
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "chapter-1",
      "title": "The useful question",
      "text": "A useful control answers three questions. What can I do here? What just changed? And how do I go back?",
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 7.4336
    },
    {
      "id": "chapter-2",
      "title": "Follow the hand",
      "text": "Imagine a dial in a quiet recording studio. Your hand moves first, the indicator follows, and the number settles in a place you already know.",
      "start": 7.9836,
      "end": 16.7407
    },
    {
      "id": "chapter-3",
      "title": "Keep the readout still",
      "text": "An interface can work the same way. Keep the readout still. Let the movement show the relationship, rather than asking someone to chase it.",
      "start": 17.2907,
      "end": 25.688
    },
    {
      "id": "chapter-4",
      "title": "Beyond the ideal moment",
      "text": "The difficult part is everything around the ideal moment. An empty track. A slow connection. A failed save. A person who cannot use a mouse.",
      "start": 26.238,
      "end": 35.2682
    },
    {
      "id": "chapter-5",
      "title": "The whole system",
      "text": "Those are not finishing touches. They are the system. Design them at the same table, with the same care as the first impression.",
      "start": 35.8182,
      "end": 43.385
    },
    {
      "id": "chapter-6",
      "title": "Let the work lead",
      "text": "When the control becomes familiar, the interface gets quieter. What remains is the work someone came here to do.",
      "start": 43.9351,
      "end": 50.729
    }
  ],
